{"id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#def:exponential-map","title":"def:exponential-map","path":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries.md","line":605,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries.tex#def:exponential-map","uses":["[[references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#def:geodesic]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries.tex","label":"def:exponential-map","line":1585,"source":"\\begin{definition}[Exponential map]\n\\label{def:exponential-map}\n\n\\uses{def:geodesic}\n\n\n\nFor any $p\\in M$, we can define the \\textit{exponential\nmap} at $p$, $\\exp_{p}$. Its natural domain $O_p$ contains the origin,\nand on that domain $\\exp_{p}(v)=\\gamma_v(1)$, the endpoint of\nthe unique geodesic $\\gamma_v\\colon [0,1]\\rightarrow M$ starting\nfrom $p$ with initial velocity vector $v$. We always take\n$O_{p}\\subset T_{p}M$ to be the maximal domain on which $\\exp_p$ is\ndefined, so that $O_p$ is a star-shaped open neighborhood of\n$0\\in T_pM$.\n\\end{definition}\n\nBy the Hopf--Rinow Theorem (Theorem~\\ref{thm:hopf-rinow} below),\nif $M$ is complete then the exponential map is defined on all of\n$T_pM$.\n\nNear the origin of $T_pM$ the exponential map is a diffeomorphism\nonto a metric ball, uniformly over compact sets of centers. (The\nforward reference to the Gauss Lemma,\nLemma~\\ref{lem:geodesic-polar-form} in the section on curvature\ncomparison below, involves no circularity: that lemma depends only\non the material of the present section and of\nSection~2.)","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[Exponential map] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a6578706f6e656e7469616c2d6d6170\"></span> <p></p><a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FMorganTian%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh01_Preliminaries%23def%3Ageodesic\">↗ def:geodesic</a> <p></p>For any <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$p\\in M$\">$p\\in M$</span>, we can define the <em>exponential map</em> at <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$p$\">$p$</span>, <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\exp _{p}$\">$\\exp _{p}$</span>. Its natural domain <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$O_p$\">$O_p$</span> contains the origin, and on that domain <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\exp _{p}(v)=\\gamma _v(1)$\">$\\exp _{p}(v)=\\gamma _v(1)$</span>, the endpoint of the unique geodesic <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\gamma _v\\colon [0,1]\\rightarrow M$\">$\\gamma _v\\colon [0,1]\\rightarrow M$</span> starting from <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$p$\">$p$</span> with initial velocity vector <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$v$\">$v$</span>. We always take <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$O_{p}\\subset T_{p}M$\">$O_{p}\\subset T_{p}M$</span> to be the maximal domain on which <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$\\exp _p$\">$\\exp _p$</span> is defined, so that <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$O_p$\">$O_p$</span> is a star-shaped open neighborhood of <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$0\\in T_pM$\">$0\\in T_pM$</span>.  <p></p>By the Hopf--Rinow Theorem (Theorem <a class=\"reference tex-ref\" href=\"#node=references%2FMorganTian%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh01_Preliminaries%23thm%3Ahopf-rinow\" data-tex-node=\"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#thm:hopf-rinow\" data-tex-anchor=\"tex-label-74686d3a686f70662d72696e6f77\">thm:hopf-rinow</a> below), if <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$M$\">$M$</span> is complete then the exponential map is defined on all of <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$T_pM$\">$T_pM$</span>. <p></p>Near the origin of <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$T_pM$\">$T_pM$</span> the exponential map is a diffeomorphism onto a metric ball, uniformly over compact sets of centers. (The forward reference to the Gauss Lemma, Lemma <a class=\"reference tex-ref\" href=\"#node=references%2FMorganTian%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh01_Preliminaries%23lem%3Ageodesic-polar-form\" data-tex-node=\"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:geodesic-polar-form\" data-tex-anchor=\"tex-label-6c656d3a67656f64657369632d706f6c61722d666f726d\">lem:geodesic-polar-form</a> in the section on curvature comparison below, involves no circularity: that lemma depends only on the material of the present section and of Section 2.)","warnings":[]},"previous":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:index-form-negative-at-conjugate","next":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:normal-ball","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#def:geodesic","title":"def:geodesic","label":"def:geodesic"}],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#modelpolar","title":"modelpolar","label":"modelpolar"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#jacmin","title":"jacmin","label":"jacmin"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:normal-ball","title":"lem:normal-ball","label":"lem:normal-ball"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:exponential-differential-jacobi","title":"lem:exponential-differential-jacobi","label":"lem:exponential-differential-jacobi"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Prelimina